  "summary": "Decorated manuscript style from early medieval Insular culture combining Christian iconography with native Celtic motifs",
  "description": "Characterized by intricate interlace, vibrant colors, and elaborate carpet pages derived from metalwork and native traditions. Developed in monastic centers as a fusion of Mediterranean and Celtic artistic traditions.",
  "culturalContext": "Insular manuscript illumination — the Hiberno-Saxon tradition (7th-9th c.) — produced the Book of Kells, Lindisfarne Gospels and Book of Durrow: carpet pages of Celtic spirals, knotwork and entangled beasts, chi-rho monogram explosions, and canon tables whose line-confident carpet geometry has never been surpassed in intricacy. Monastic scriptoria fused Pictish metalwork logic with gospel text; the style's zoomorphic interlace remains the West's most recognizable medieval graphic identity.",
